# Contains Duplicate

## Intuition

We need to check whether any value appears more than once in the array.

We can use a hash table to store the elements that we have already seen. While traversing the array, if an element is already present in the hash table, then a duplicate exists.

Since C does not have a built-in hash set, we implement the hash table using an array of linked lists to handle collisions.

## Approach

1. Create a hash table of size `100003`.
2. Traverse every element of the array.
3. Calculate the hash index using:
   `abs(nums[i]) % SIZE`
4. Search the linked list at that index.
5. If the value is already present, return `true`.
6. Otherwise, create a new node and insert the value into the hash table.
7. If no duplicate is found, return `false`.

## Complexity

- Time complexity: $$O(n)$$ average case
- Space complexity: $$O(n)$$

## Code

```c
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int val;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

#define SIZE 100003

bool containsDuplicate(int* nums, int numsSize) {
    Node* hash[SIZE] = {0};

    for(int i = 0; i < numsSize; i++) {

        int key = abs(nums[i]) % SIZE;

        Node* curr = hash[key];

        while(curr) {
            if(curr->val == nums[i])
                return true;

            curr = curr->next;
        }

        Node* newNode = (Node*)malloc(sizeof(Node));
        newNode->val = nums[i];
        newNode->next = hash[key];
        hash[key] = newNode;
    }

    return false;
}